\section{A Context Is Part of the Type of Suspended Code}

A type-and-effect judgment can say that evaluating an expression produces a value of type $A$ while possibly invoking operations from $\Psi$. That fact is different from saying that the expression is already a value representing a suspended computation. Nikita Zyuzin and Aleksandar Nanevski's \emph{Contextual Modal Types for Algebraic Effects and Handlers} gives suspended computations a type $[\Psi]A$, where $\Psi$ is a context of operation signatures. The introduction form $\mathsf{box}\,\Psi.\,c$ binds those operation names in $c$. Forming the value does not invoke the operations; using the underlying computation requires an interpretation of its context. This makes the stage at which effects can occur visible in the term language, not just in an annotation.

The same logical idea can describe other kinds of dependency. In Tom Murphy VII's \emph{Modal Types for Mobile Code}, worlds are associated with places in distributed computation, so types can track where resources are meaningful. An operation context is not a network location, and boxing does not make a local resource transportable. The comparison is useful because both systems force the implementation to distinguish a value available now from computation whose assumptions must be met elsewhere or later. For the ordinary type-and-effect view, see Chapter~\ref{ch:effect-systems}.

\section{Worked Handler: Thread One Integer Cell}

Let $\mathsf{St}$ be the context with operations $\mathsf{get}:\mathsf{Unit}\to\mathsf{Int}$ and $\mathsf{put}:\mathsf{Int}\to\mathsf{Unit}$. In schematic syntax, the following suspended computation returns the old state:
\begin{verbatim}
inc = box St.
  x <- get()
  _ <- put(x + 1)
  return x
\end{verbatim}
Thus $\mathsf{inc} : [\mathsf{St}]\mathsf{Int}$. A state handler can eliminate these operations by passing a state $s$ explicitly. Each operation clause receives a continuation $k$ for the rest of the handled computation. Write $k(v,s')$ for resuming with operation result $v$ and new handler state $s'$. The three clauses are
\[
\begin{aligned}
  \mathsf{get}((),k,s) &\mapsto k(s,s),\\
  \mathsf{put}(n,k,s) &\mapsto k((),n),\\
  \mathsf{return}(v,s) &\mapsto (v,s).
\end{aligned}
\]
Unbox $\mathsf{inc}$ and run it with this handler from state $4$. The first clause resumes with value $4$ and state $4$. The second resumes with unit and state $5$. The return clause yields $(4,5)$. No physical mutable cell was needed. A different handler for $\mathsf{St}$ could even translate the operations into another nonempty context: it would then have to account for its own residual operations. Unboxing alone binds the suspended body for later use; it is not a monadic sequencing step that runs the body immediately.

\Cref{fig:visual-147} tracks the operation result separately
from the threaded state; in particular, writing \(5\) does not
return \(5\) to the suspended program.

