The operation result type and the handler's answer type are different. The resumption at $\mathsf{get}$ takes an integer, the one at $\mathsf{put}$ takes unit, and both resume a computation whose final handled answer is an integer pair. Giving all resumptions the same argument type because they belong to the same handler would reject one of these clauses or silently lose typing information in the IR.

\section{Keep Handler Scope in the Intermediate Language}

The lowering invariant is that a performed operation resolves to its lexical binding and the correct active interpretation, with an argument and resumption compatible with the operation signature. An explicit representation can pass a handler environment and represent resumptions using continuations or delimited stack segments. Inlining the state handler may turn the two operations above into ordinary state-threading code. Inlining the suspended body at a point with a different handler, or running it once at its binding and sharing the result, is not the same transformation. Either could change the result or when an effect occurs.

An empty operation context only rules out the operations tracked by the chosen calculus. It is not a general guarantee of termination or of constant cost. A one-shot stack representation also needs an independent restriction or proof that continuations will not be resumed more than once; the state example calls each continuation once, but other handlers can discard or duplicate resumptions. Test lowering with nested handlers for distinct bindings of the same operation name and with a box that is never run. Markdown Dossier Chapter~16 considers effect-aware IR more broadly; here the essential information is the scope and interpretation of operations, not just a set of effect names.
